A||||6||Clint||Quad 4 from where?||||5
Z||000000||Clint||09-29-1999||07:45 AM||||I'm to the point where i want to pick up an engine for my 1st Fiero. (i'm driving my 3rd at the moment)  I've pretty much decided on the Quad 4 for numerous reasons. (easier to cool, better fit, better gas mileage,hopefully cheaper at the yards.)  What car should I be looking to pull this out of?  It has to be a manual transmission. and I am looking for the most Torque possible.  HP is a consideration, but I prefer the "back in the seat" torque.<P>So my question is what years, and what cars. i.e. a 91 beretta? a 92 olds?<P>thanks<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||ChadMan||09-29-1999||08:57 AM||||I have done a little research into Quad 4's.  The general consensus among the mechanics and Fierophiles I've talked to is that the early ones (88-90) had a chronic problem with blowing head gaskets.  The newer ones are supposed to be more reliable, especially the 1992's and newer.<P>Supposedly, when GM redesigned the Quad-4 and renamed it "2.4 Twin Cam," it became even more reliable.  (This is the motor found in newer Z24's and Sunfire GT's.)  This motor makes a lot more torque than the original Quad (even the H.O.) but sacrifices HP (150 vs. 160, 180 or 190 depending on configuration).  Also, it's an OBD-II motor with a much more complex emissions-control system, and it might be a bitch to make it work in a Fiero.<P>The "Holy Grail" of Quad-4's is the 190-hp unit found in the limited-edition Olds Achieva SCX.  But good luck finding one - Olds built these cars with an eye on winning Firehawk Endurance racing and these cars are really, really rare.  Someone said this motor can also be found in the Olds Quad 442 with the W-31 package (also really rare).||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||09-29-1999||02:57 PM||||HO Quads in the Cutlass Calais International too.||||reg||1
Z||000003||88 Fiero||09-29-1999||03:21 PM||||Chad is right!!! most common problems on Quad 4's are head gasket problems...||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||09-29-1999||09:04 PM||||Clint, if you want a bunch of low end torque, then I suggest looking elsewhere because the Quad-4 is known for it's lack of torque. Why do you think they rarely get mated with an automatic. For a 4 cylinder, they are a high reving motor and make a fair amount of hp at the upper end. The rule of thumb for torque is "there's no replacement for displacement." This means larger engines make more torque. One last thing, go out and price head work, cams, and other valve train parts. I think you'll go into shock. Everyone I've known with these engines had trouble with them. I'm referring to the 2.3L, I have heard the newer redesigned 2.4L is somewhat better.||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||09-29-1999||10:29 PM||||I agree. The DOHC's and the Quad 4's are very low on low end torque. For street racing, they just don't cut it. Buick.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Eddie||09-29-1999||10:42 PM||||I MOST SAY SAVE YOUR MONEY GET V6 TRUST ME <BR>Sorry to be so neg. but it,s true.||||reg||1
